<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/10/27/apple-creator-studio-final-cut-pro-motionvfx-261027-1793113245739-88898398dc.jpg" alt="Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p2">Today, Apple released one of its biggest updates to <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/final-cut-pro-create-video/id1631624924" rel="noopener noreferrer">Final Cut Pro</a> since the app became part of <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app-bundle/apple-creator-studio/id1868448255?mt=12" rel="noopener noreferrer">Creator Studio</a>. The marquee addition is thousands of MotionVFX themes, transitions, effects, and more. But there’s more to it than just that, so let’s take a look.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/17/apple-creator-studio-final-cut-pro-with-pixelmator-pro-261027-1794936914077-5f0f0c15cb.jpg" alt="Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p4">When <a href="https://www.macstories.net/linked/motionvfx-team-joins-apple/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple acquired MotionVFX</a> in March, it was a big deal because the Poland-based company’s highly regarded tools for Final Cut Pro had been a go-to resource for YouTubers and other creators for years. Those tools were also expensive. Now, that bounty of titles, transitions, effects, and more is all available inside Final Cut Pro as part of a Creator Studio subscription. That’s a big deal for creators who paid <a href="https://maxfrequency.net/2026/10/27/apple-motionvfx-creator-studio-merged/" rel="noopener noreferrer">as much as $350/year for a MotionVFX subscription</a>, and can get it all and more with a $129 Creator Studio annual (or $12.99/month) subscription.</p>
<p id="p5">The update introduces the Content Hub from other Creator Studio apps for the first time, too. In addition to the existing stock photography and graphics, the Content Hub now includes video you can use in Final Cut Pro or other projects. Images used in Final Cut Pro projects can also be round-tripped to <a href="https://apps.apple.com/us/app/pixelmator-pro-edit-images/id6746662575" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pixelmator Pro</a> for editing.</p>
<div class="media-wrapper"><img decoding="async" src="https://cdn.macstories.net/images/uploads/2026/11/17/apple-creator-studio-final-cut-pro-content-hub-261027-1794936909760-6476ccc267.jpg" alt="Source: Apple."><p class="image-caption">Source: Apple.</p></div>
<p id="p7">Other updates include:</p>
<ul id="ul8"><li>improved keyframing for precisely controlling animation,</li>
<li>more transcription options for more languages,</li>
<li>the ability to detach clips from the timeline, which may help users who find the Magnetic Timeline hard to get used to, and</li>
<li>advanced image generation with AI.</li>
</ul><p id="p9">Many of these features are things I’ll probably never use in my podcast production pipeline, but I’m glad they’re there. There are plenty of other features of the Creator Studio suite that I use every week that make its subscription worth it to me already. What’s exciting is that now for no additional cost, I can start experimenting with MotionVFX, something I’ve always wanted to try, but didn’t want to pay a separate subscription for simply tinkering. I expect there are a lot of people in the same boat. Even if you won’t try some of these features right away, simply having them at your disposal means it’s easier to explore new creative workflows.</p>
<p id="p10">Creator Studio is available on the App Store for $12.99/month or $129/year and includes Final Cut Pro, Logic Pro, Motion, Compressor, Soundstage, Pixelmator Pro, Freeform, Numbers, Pages, and Keynote.</p>
